<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Fake News Detection</h3>

<section id="S2.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.1. </span>Unimodal Methods</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Text-based approaches focus on different analytical dimensions. Some methods integrate social context by leveraging propaganda strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, user feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, temporal trends <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and news environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sheng et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Ghanem et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghanem et al., 2021</a>)</cite> proposed a technique that combines topic and sentiment features extracted from textual data. More recently, Nan et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nan et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and Zhu et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> explored domain shift issues caused by variations in word frequency and sentiment, introducing domain gating and domain memory mechanisms to improve domain representation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Various image-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> have been introduced to assess the authenticity of visual content by analyzing edited traces. Some CNN-based techniques operate in the spatial domain to identify artifact patterns, utilizing approaches such as blending <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2020a</a>)</cite>, multiple instance learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2020b</a>)</cite>, patch consistency analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, reconstruction techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and local feature mining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Alternatively, other studies focus on transforming images into the frequency domain through methods like discrete cosine transform <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qian et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, composite phase spectrum analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and high-frequency noise extraction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.1.2. </span>Multimodal Methods</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mainstream multimodal MFND methods extract semantic representations by integrating cross-modal textual and visual features <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaiswal et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. Some approaches enhance semantic information by incorporating external knowledge bases, as proposed by Sabir et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sabir et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and Wang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Qi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qi et al., 2021</a>)</cite> focused on extracting visual entities to improve high-level news comprehension. Peng et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2017</a>)</cite> applied named entity recognition and word segmentation to enhance Chinese social media identification. To better fuse textual and visual features, co-attention networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and contextual attention networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qian et al., 2021</a>)</cite> have been developed. Kochkina et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zubiaga et al., 2017</a>)</cite> employed a multi-task learning approach for rumor verification. Additionally, Ying et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ying et al., 2023</a>)</cite> introduced an improved Mixture of Experts network to refine feature integration across multiple perspectives. To mitigate spurious correlations from modality inconsistencies and data biases, ambiguity learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and causal inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite> were introduced separately. FAK-Owl <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> performs knowledge-enhanced verification based on Large Vision-Language Models. NSLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>)</cite> utilizes neuro-symbolic reasoning for misinformation detection. MIAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> is an attention network based on the utilization of intrinsic discriminative features. DGA-FAKE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> detects fake news early through the diffusion propagation path. Recent work MIMoE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> detects misinformation through interactive mixture of experts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These methods effectively utilize image and text modalities to uncover implicit misinformation but struggle to handle missing modalities. Since such issues frequently occur in real-world applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Melumad et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, we propose MMLNet, a robust and practical approach for detecting misinformation in news.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Robust Multimodal Learning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The issue of missing modalities is prevalent across all types of multimodal data and has been widely studied. Early research employed data imputation methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lian et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to recover missing modalities from available ones. Some approaches directly fill in missing modalities with fixed values <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Zhang, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Additionally, some researchers leverage the generative capabilities of specific neural network architectures, such as autoencoders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vincent et al., 2008</a>)</cite> and Transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vaswani et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2023</a>)</cite> mitigate incomplete modalities through prompt learning. Some recovery-based methods
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, have been proven to be effective in robust multimodal learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, these modality recovery-based methods are not well-suited for MFND, as they inherently rely on modality consistency. Misinformation is often embedded in modality inconsistencies, making modality recovery detrimental to the model’s performance. Therefore, we utilize the modal feature distribution learned from multiple perspectives by MMLNet, make up for the missing features with the invisible feature distribution through the incomplete modality adapter, and effectively enhance the robustness of the modal features through modality missing learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Method</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Problem Definition</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.1. </span>Multimodal Fake News Detection</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MFND task is a typical binary classification problem, where the positive class corresponds to <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fake news</span> and the negative class corresponds to <span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">real news</span>.
Given a multi-modal news dataset <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{x_{i},y_{i}\mid x_{i}=(x_{i}^{T},x_{i}^{V})\}_{i=1}^{N}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{x_{i},y_{i}\mid x_{i}=(x_{i}^{T},x_{i}^{V})\}_{i=1}^{N}.</annotation></semantics></math>
<math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>represents textual information, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}^{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}^{V}</annotation></semantics></math>represents visual information, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the corresponding label. To effectively distinguish fake news, the model’s objective is to learn the probability distribution of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(y\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(y\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.1.2. </span>Incomplete Modality</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We focus on fine-grained modality incompleteness by preprocessing the raw data to obtain incomplete modality data. The text modality exhibits incompleteness at the word-level, while the image modality is incomplete at the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> patch-level. Specifically, given a news text <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{T}=\{t_{1},t_{2},\dots,t_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{T}=\{t_{1},t_{2},\dots,t_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th word and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sentence length. We introduce text modality incompleteness by randomly removing <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{T}\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{T}\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the words. The remaining text contains <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{m}=m\times(1-M^{T}\%)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{m}=m\times(1-M^{T}\%)</annotation></semantics></math> words, represented as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}^{T}=\{t_{1},t_{2},\dots,t_{\hat{m}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>m</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}^{T}=\{t_{1},t_{2},\dots,t_{\hat{m}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding news image is represented as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{V}=\{v_{1},v_{2},\dots,v_{n\times n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{V}=\{v_{1},v_{2},\dots,v_{n\times n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the image is divided into <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> patches, and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th patch. Under an image missing rate of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{V}\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{V}\%</annotation></semantics></math>, we randomly mask <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{V}\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{V}\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the image patches, leaving <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{n}=n\times n\times(1-M^{V}\%)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{n}=n\times n\times(1-M^{V}\%)</annotation></semantics></math> valid patches, denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}^{V}=\{v_{1},v_{2},\dots,v_{\hat{n}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>V</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>n</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}^{V}=\{v_{1},v_{2},\dots,v_{\hat{n}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, the newly constructed incomplete multimodal news data is represented as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}=\{\hat{x}^{T},\hat{x}^{V}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>V</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}=\{\hat{x}^{T},\hat{x}^{V}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, under the incomplete modality setting, our new optimization objective is formulated using maximum likelihood estimation, expressed as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max\mathcal{O}=\mathbb{E}_{\left(\hat{x},y\right)\sim p_{\text{Train }}}\log p\left(y\mid\hat{x}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>Train </mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max\mathcal{O}=\mathbb{E}_{\left(\hat{x},y\right)\sim p_{\text{Train }}}\log p\left(y\mid\hat{x}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{train}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the distribution of incomplete modalities in the training data.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05839v1/model.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/217;" width="476" height="217" alt="A description of your image.">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>. </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of MMLNet framework.</span><span id="acmlabel2" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">A description of your image.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Overview Framework</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.1.2. Incomplete Modality ‣ 3.1. Problem Definition ‣ 3. Method ‣ Towards Robust and Realible Multimodal Fake News Detection with Incomplete Modality" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the overall architecture of our proposed MMLNet consists of three key components designed for incomplete modalities: a Modality-specific Multi-expert Reasoning paradigm, an Incomplete Modality Adapter module, and a Modality missing learning module.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Multi-Expert Reasoning Module</h3>

<section id="S3.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.1. </span>Expert of Text-Modality</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In multimodal learning, the text modality typically plays a dominant role due to its higher information density. An incomplete text modality significantly impacts model performance; therefore, modeling text modality experts is crucial for the MFND task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With the multimodal input, we adopt the CLIP model (Contrastive Language-Image Pretraining) , a vision-and-language pre-trained model that has demonstrated significant performance across various vision-and-language downstream tasks , to extract features of the textual modality and the image modality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a news text <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{T}=\{t_{1},t_{2},\dots,t_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{T}=\{t_{1},t_{2},\dots,t_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th word and <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sentence length. The CLIP text encoder <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕋</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{T}</annotation></semantics></math> outputs the corresponding representation <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H=\mathbb{T}(x^{T})=({h}_{\text{{[CLS]}}},{h}_{1},{h}_{2},...,{h}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝕋</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H=\mathbb{T}(x^{T})=({h}_{\text{{[CLS]}}},{h}_{1},{h}_{2},...,{h}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We employ <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{h}_{\texttt{{[CLS]}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">[CLS]</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{h}_{\texttt{{[CLS]}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to model the distribution of the text modality expert, and we employ the text modality adapter <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{A}^{T}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{A}^{T}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to enhance feature robustness, as shown below:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{T}=f_{A}^{T}(h_{\text{{[CLS]}}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{T}=f_{A}^{T}(h_{\text{{[CLS]}}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{y}^{h}=\text{softmax}(\boldsymbol{W}F_{T}+\boldsymbol{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{y}^{h}=\text{softmax}(\boldsymbol{W}F_{T}+\boldsymbol{b})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{y}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{y}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is the output distribution of text modality expert.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.2. </span>Expert of Image-Modality</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The image modality is typically a complementary modality to text, and its integration with the text modality facilitates the assessment of news authenticity. We employ the CLIP image encoder to model the image modality expert.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the corresponding image <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{V}=\{v_{1},v_{2},\dots,v_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{V}=\{v_{1},v_{2},\dots,v_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the CLIP image encoder <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}</annotation></semantics></math> outputs the corresponding representation <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R=\mathbb{V}(x^{V})=({r}_{\text{{[CLS]}}},{r}_{1},{r}_{2},...,{r}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝕍</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>V</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R=\mathbb{V}(x^{V})=({r}_{\text{{[CLS]}}},{r}_{1},{r}_{2},...,{r}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> represents the number of image patches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{r}_{\text{{[CLS]}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐫</mi><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{r}_{\text{{[CLS]}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to model the image modality expert, and we employ the image modality adapter <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{A}^{I}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>I</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{A}^{I}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to enhance feature robustness, as shown below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{I}=f_{A}^{I}({r}_{\text{{[CLS]}}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>I</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{I}=f_{A}^{I}({r}_{\text{{[CLS]}}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{y}^{r}=\text{softmax}(\boldsymbol{W}F_{I}+\boldsymbol{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>I</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{y}^{r}=\text{softmax}(\boldsymbol{W}F_{I}+\boldsymbol{b})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y}^{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y}^{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is the output distribution of image modality expert.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05839v1/figcl.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/124;" width="476" height="124" alt="A description of your image.">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>. </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution of features in space for complete and incomplete modality.</span><span id="acmlabel3" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">A description of your image.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.3. </span>Expert of Multi-Modality</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modeling the distribution between text modality and image modality is advantageous for capturing deep-level deceptive information. However, incomplete modality information can mislead the modeling of inter-modality distributions, thereby hindering the detection of deceptive information. In this work, we employ a transformer encoder to model the joint expert distribution across multiple modalities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, given the yielded text representation <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and image representation <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, we first concat them as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F=(H,R)=(h_{\text{[CLS]}},h_{1},\dots,h_{m},r_{\text{[CLS]}},r_{1},\dots,r_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F=(H,R)=(h_{\text{[CLS]}},h_{1},\dots,h_{m},r_{\text{[CLS]}},r_{1},\dots,r_{n})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Then, we apply different linear functions to obtain the corresponding queries <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, keys <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> and values <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, and the updated representation <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{F}</annotation></semantics></math> can be denoted as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{F}=(\hat{h}_{\text{[CLS]}},\hat{h}_{1},\dots,\hat{h}_{m},\hat{r}_{\text{[CLS]}},\hat{r}_{1},\dots,\hat{r}_{n})=\text{softmax}\left(\frac{QK^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\right)V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{F}=(\hat{h}_{\text{[CLS]}},\hat{h}_{1},\dots,\hat{h}_{m},\hat{r}_{\text{[CLS]}},\hat{r}_{1},\dots,\hat{r}_{n})=\text{softmax}\left(\frac{QK^{\top}}{\sqrt{d_{k}}}\right)V</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is the mapped dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">we use attention mechanism to further fuse the image-text interaction feature <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> by calculating:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{t},p_{v}=\text{softmax}(\boldsymbol{W}(\hat{h}_{\text{[CLS]}},\hat{r}_{\text{[CLS]}})+b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{t},p_{v}=\text{softmax}(\boldsymbol{W}(\hat{h}_{\text{[CLS]}},\hat{r}_{\text{[CLS]}})+b)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f=p_{t}\hat{h}_{\text{[CLS]}}+p_{v}\hat{r}_{\text{[CLS]}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f=p_{t}\hat{h}_{\text{[CLS]}}+p_{v}\hat{r}_{\text{[CLS]}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y^{f}=\text{softmax}(\boldsymbol{W}f+\boldsymbol{b})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y^{f}=\text{softmax}(\boldsymbol{W}f+\boldsymbol{b})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is output distribution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.3.4. </span>Multi-Expert Reasoning Paradigm</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the obtained expert feature distributions <math id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{h},y^{r},y^{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{h},y^{r},y^{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, We employ a dynamic routing network to balance the weights of different expert distributions, aiming to better integrate the judgments of various experts in determining the authenticity of news.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{y}^{o}=\sum_{M\in\left\{h,r,f\right\}}{\lambda}_{o}^{M}{y}^{M},{\lambda}_{o}\in\left[0,1\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{y}^{o}=\sum_{M\in\left\{h,r,f\right\}}{\lambda}_{o}^{M}{y}^{M},{\lambda}_{o}\in\left[0,1\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learnable parameter, <math id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>o</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{o}</annotation></semantics></math> can be regarded as harnessing rich features from the text modality <math id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>, image modality <math id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, and image-text interaction modality <math id="S3.SS3.SSS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, which enables MMLNet to achieve better robustness in the presence of missing modality information.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4. </span>Incomplete Modality Adapters</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mainstream MFND tasks typically employ models pre-trained on large-scale datasets as encoders, which may lead to overfitting of feature distributions, a problem that becomes more pronounced when modality information is missing. Inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, we introduce lightweight feature adapters for single-modality image and text, enabling the learning of new modality features to compensate for the missing information while preserving the original distributions learned by the pre-trained models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We utilize residual connections to merge the feature distributions of images and text with the new distributions from adapters, enabling the model to balance compensated modality features and disentangled image and text features. With the image and text Feature Adapters <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{I}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>I</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{I}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{T}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{T}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, the final unimodal representations <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{I},F_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{I},F_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{I}=\alpha A^{I}({r}_{\text{{[CLS]}}})+(1-\alpha){r}_{\text{{[CLS]}}}=f_{A}^{I}({r}_{\text{{[CLS]}}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>I</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>I</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{I}=\alpha A^{I}({r}_{\text{{[CLS]}}})+(1-\alpha){r}_{\text{{[CLS]}}}=f_{A}^{I}({r}_{\text{{[CLS]}}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{T}=\alpha A^{T}({h}_{\text{{[CLS]}}})+(1-\alpha){h}_{\text{{[CLS]}}}=f_{A}^{T}({h}_{\text{{[CLS]}}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mtext>[CLS]</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{T}=\alpha A^{T}({h}_{\text{{[CLS]}}})+(1-\alpha){h}_{\text{{[CLS]}}}=f_{A}^{T}({h}_{\text{{[CLS]}}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is residual ratio to maintain harmony between these two modules.
We do not focus on compensating for multimodal feature distributions; instead, we aim to extract misinformation from interactions within the original single modality features.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>Modality Missing Learning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3.2. Expert of Image-Modality ‣ 3.3. Multi-Expert Reasoning Module ‣ 3. Method ‣ Towards Robust and Realible Multimodal Fake News Detection with Incomplete Modality" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, intuitively, when samples with opposite labels show greater semantic similarity, it is more difficult for the model to distinguish the authenticity of the news. On the contrary, when there is a significant difference in the semantic similarity of samples with the same label, the model tends to make inconsistent inferences about their authenticity. Missing modality information worsens this phenomenon.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To enhance the performance of MMLNet in MFND tasks under conditions of incomplete modality information, we propose a modality missing learning algorithm based on a vanilla multimodal contrastive learning framework. This simple yet effective approach aims to mitigate the negative effects of missing modality information by constraining the feature distributions across multiple modalities.
Specifically, for each training sample <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, we first define the corresponding positive sample set <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and negative sample set <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for it. Here, <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> contains samples with the same label as <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> contains samples with the opposite label as <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3.2. Expert of Image-Modality ‣ 3.3. Multi-Expert Reasoning Module ‣ 3. Method ‣ Towards Robust and Realible Multimodal Fake News Detection with Incomplete Modality" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we aim to learn a robust latent representation of <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> by attracting <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to the representation of samples in <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and repidating it away from the representation of samples in <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{m}=\frac{1}{\left|\mathcal{S}_{p}\right|}\sum_{p\in\mathcal{S}_{p}}-\log\frac{\exp\left(f(\mathbf{h})\cdot f\left(\mathbf{h}_{p}\right)/\tau\right)}{\sum_{n\in\mathcal{S}_{n}}\exp\left(f(\mathbf{h})\cdot f\left(\mathbf{h}_{n}\right)/\tau\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{m}=\frac{1}{\left|\mathcal{S}_{p}\right|}\sum_{p\in\mathcal{S}_{p}}-\log\frac{\exp\left(f(\mathbf{h})\cdot f\left(\mathbf{h}_{p}\right)/\tau\right)}{\sum_{n\in\mathcal{S}_{n}}\exp\left(f(\mathbf{h})\cdot f\left(\mathbf{h}_{n}\right)/\tau\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes an MLP projection. <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> is a temperature coefficient. <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are the final representations of the positive sample <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and negative sample <math id="S3.SS5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">However, in incomplete modal MFND task, it is suboptimal to weight all positive and negative samples equally. This alignment objective is not fully compatible with multimodal fake news detection with incomplete modalities. Based on the aforementioned perceptions, our model encounters two common but difficult problems caused by the lack of modality information: 1) distinguishing samples with similar semantic information but opposite labels due to incomplete modal information, and 2) narrowing the gap between samples with different semantic information but the same label due to incomplete modal information. To accomplish this, we introduce a label-aware adaptive weighting strategy. Specifically, we measure the semantic similarity of two samples by measuring the cosine similarity, each sample is re-weighted as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.EGx13.1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.EGx13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w_{c}=\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}1-cos(h_{c},h),\left(c\in\mathcal{S}_{p}\right)\\
1+cos(h_{c},h),\left(c\in\mathcal{S}_{n}\right)\end{array}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w_{c}=\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}1-cos(h_{c},h),\left(c\in\mathcal{S}_{p}\right)\\
1+cos(h_{c},h),\left(c\in\mathcal{S}_{n}\right)\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the label-aware weight for sample <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the representation vector of sample <math id="S3.SS5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>. Ultimately, the contrastive loss is refined as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(19)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{m}=\frac{1}{\left|\mathcal{S}_{p}\right|}\sum_{p\in\mathcal{S}_{p}}-\log\frac{w_{p}\cdot\exp\left(f(\mathbf{h})\cdot f\left(\mathbf{h}_{p}\right)/\tau\right)}{\sum_{n\in\mathcal{S}_{n}}w_{n}\cdot\exp\left(f(\mathbf{h})\cdot f\left(\mathbf{h}_{n}\right)/\tau\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\mathcal{L}}_{m}=\frac{1}{\left|\mathcal{S}_{p}\right|}\sum_{p\in\mathcal{S}_{p}}-\log\frac{w_{p}\cdot\exp\left(f(\mathbf{h})\cdot f\left(\mathbf{h}_{p}\right)/\tau\right)}{\sum_{n\in\mathcal{S}_{n}}w_{n}\cdot\exp\left(f(\mathbf{h})\cdot f\left(\mathbf{h}_{n}\right)/\tau\right)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p9.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the weights assigned for a positive sample <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and a negative sample <math id="S3.SS5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, modality missing learning is not only effective for multimodal features. We also apply it to text modality features <math id="S3.SS5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and image modality features <math id="S3.SS5.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> to further constrain the distribution of unimodal features and enhance the robustness of the MMLNet model in the incomplete modality MFND task.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6. </span>Overall Optimization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ the cross-entropy loss to optimize as follows,</p>
<table id="S3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{c}=-\sum_{i=1}^{N}y_{i}^{T}~log~\hat{y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>c</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{c}=-\sum_{i=1}^{N}y_{i}^{T}~log~\hat{y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> represents the ground truth, <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}</annotation></semantics></math> stands for the probability of the predicted label corresponding to the i-th image-text pair.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we combine the classification and modality missing learning loss functions together to optimize the whole model as follows,</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(21)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}=\sum_{M\in\{I,T,F\}}\lambda_{o}^{M}\left(\lambda_{c}\mathcal{L}_{c}^{M}+\lambda_{m}\mathcal{L}_{m}^{M}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>M</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}=\sum_{M\in\{I,T,F\}}\lambda_{o}^{M}\left(\lambda_{c}\mathcal{L}_{c}^{M}+\lambda_{m}\mathcal{L}_{m}^{M}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter that controls the loss weight to balance learning. Note that the hyperparameter <math id="S3.SS6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{o}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{o}</annotation></semantics></math> is consistent with Eq. 13.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we conduct relevant experiments to address several research questions. Through these experimental results, we systematically evaluate the effectiveness of MMLNet in tackling key challenges in incomplete modality fake news detection:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1:</span> Can MMLNet effectively enhance the performance of fake news detection?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2:</span> Is the proposed design robust for fake news detection under incomplete modalities?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3:</span> Do individual components contribute to improving fake news detection capability?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ4:</span> Can modallity missing learning significantly improve incomplete modality MFND?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ5:</span> How do MMLNet accurately assess the authenticity of the news?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ6:</span> Does MMLNet have limitations in determining the authenticity of news with incomplete information?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Datasets</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the performance of MMLNet on three real-world datasets: Weibo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, Weibo21 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nan et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and Pheme <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zubiaga et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, covering both Chinese and English. The Weibo dataset is the most widely used Chinese fake news detection dataset, containing fake and real news from Sina Weibo between 2012 and 2016, verified by authoritative sources such as Xinhua News Agency and Sina’s official debunking system. The Weibo21 dataset, released in 2021, is an updated version of the Weibo dataset that includes more recent Weibo news. Pheme is an English-language dataset consisting of tweets from Twitter, focusing on five breaking news events. Each event comprises a set of posts containing a substantial amount of textual and visual content, along with corresponding annotations. Three events are randomly selected, and their associated tweets are used for training, while tweets from the remaining events are utilized for testing. For comparison with baseline methods, we follow previous studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dong et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and use the same preprocessed dataset.</p>
</div>
